  "image_name": "34a0cdc8a1ae0deb6a570ecda61913ba.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how human activities release toxic metals into the environment and move them into agricultural land and water. It links mining, industry, energy production, transportation, and farming in one connected system. Ore extraction, smelting, cement manufacturing, waste incineration, and fossil-fuel power plants emit pollutants to the air, which are then redistributed by atmospheric deposition and rainfall. Mining waste and industrial areas also generate tailings, runoff, and wastewater that flow into streams and rivers.\n\nThe diagram emphasizes that these contaminants can reach cropland through several pathways: irrigation with contaminated water, application of sewage sludge, fertilizer use, and manure. Labels for metals such as Hg, As, Pb, Cd, Cr, and Cu appear at different points, showing that multiple elements are involved and that different sources contribute different metals. Vehicles and industrial facilities also appear to add emissions near farmland.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that heavy metal contamination is not from a single source but from overlapping anthropogenic inputs. Air, water, and soil pathways interact, allowing pollutants released by industry, mining, and agriculture to accumulate in fields and nearby aquatic systems.",
